]}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Air set in motion by breathing; breath; hence, sometimes, life itself.", "tags": ["countable", "obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "For else he sure had left not one aliue,>br>But all in his reuenge of spirite would depriue.", "reference": "1596, Edmund Spenser, “Book V, Canto VII”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC, stanza 36:"}, {"text": "But the milde air with season moderate / Gently attempred, and disposd so well, / That still it breathed forth sweet spirit & holesome smell.", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book II, Canto XII”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC, stanza 51:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) A rough breathing; an aspirate, such as the letter h; also, a mark denoting aspiration.", "tags": ["countable", "obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Be it a letter or spirit, we have great use of it.", "reference": "1640, Ben Jonson, The English Grammar:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(alchemy, obsolete) Any of the four substances: sulphur, sal ammoniac, quicksilver, and arsenic (or, according to some, orpiment).", "tags": ["countable", "obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(dyeing) Stannic chloride.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(philosophy, Hegelian) The essence behind historical development of both individual and society evolving towards the Absolute.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["spirit", "liqueur", "liquor", "apparition", "specter", "vision"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈspɪɹɪt/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈspɪɹɪt/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈspiɹɪt/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈspɪɾɪt/", "tags": ["Scotland", "Wales"]}], "forms": [{"word": "spirits", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "spiriting", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "spirited", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "spirited", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "spir't", "tags": ["alternative", "Early", "Modern"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To carry off, especially in haste, secrecy, or mystery.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I felt as if I had been spirited into some castle of felicity.", "reference": "1835, Nathaniel Parker Willis, Pencillings by the Way:"}, {"text": "When I was young and full of grace, I sprited a rattlesnake.\nWhen I was young, a fever fell my spirit; I will not tell.\nYou're on your honor not to tell.", "reference": "1986 July 28, R.E.M., “I Believe”, in Lifes Rich Pageant:"}, {"text": "God does not make an appearance, but the Devil (Ms. Pinal) emphatically does: first in the guise of a schoolgirl who tries to lure Simon down with the sight of her shapely legs; then as a bearded but blatantly female Jesus carrying a lamb; and finally as a stylishly coiffed woman who succeeds in spiriting Simon off, by means of a jet, to a Manhattan discotheque — Buñuel’s persuasive idea of hell.", "reference": "2009 February 8, Dave Kehr, “Buñuel at His Wildest, in Circulation Again”, in New York Times:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Sometimes followed by up: to animate with vigour; to excite; to encourage; to inspirit.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Civil dissensions often spirit the ambition of private men."], "quotes": [{"text": "[H]e left behind many Officers and private Men, vvho novv ſpirit-up and aſſiſt thoſe obſtinate People to continue in their Rebellion.", "reference": "1714 February, [Jonathan Swift], The Publick Spirit of the Whigs: Set forth in Their Generous Encouragement of the Author of the Crisis: […], London: […] [John Barber] for John Morphew, […], →OCLC, page 39:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spirit", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}